  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_042 (scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) achieved 0.715 accuracy at 4.49 GPU hrs, a large jump over config_001 (resnet50, 0.631 at 7.27 hrs), strongly indicating imagenet_vit_l is the superior image encoder. The only remaining major untested dimension in this exact architecture is the RNA encoder. Question: Does replacing the learned 200-dim scvi_n200 representation with a 2000-dim linear PCA projection (pca_n2000) improve, match, or degrade performance when paired with the strong ViT-L image encoder? This is a clean single-variable A/B test against config_042, isolating the RNA encoder's contribution. Why this is the best next experiment: The RNA encoder is a fundamental representation choice that could interact with the image modality; if PCA works comparably it would be cheaper and simpler, while if scvi is clearly better we confirm the learned representation's value before investing in more complex aggregation/fusion variants. Expected cost: similar to config_042 (~4–5 GPU hrs) since PCA requires no encoder training and the ViT-L backbone is identical, making this a low-cost, high-information experiment."
}

